  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis pinpoints **Tryptophan metabolism (KEGG map00380)** as the dominant affected pathway, with an FDR of 0.0 and a striking fold-enrichment of 667, with six of the nine input metabolites mapping to it. The matched compounds span the full spectrum of tryptophan catabolism: L-tryptophan (C00078, the entry-point substrate), the neurotransmitters serotonin (C00780) and melatonin (C01598), the trace amine tryptamine (C00398), and the two neuroactive kynurenine-pathway intermediates quinolinic acid (C03722) and 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid (C00632). Pathway membership was confirmed for L-tryptophan, serotonin, and quinolinic acid via direct lookup, providing a solid evidentiary basis for the narrative.\n\nA directed KEGG reaction-graph query confirms that **L-tryptophan is upstream of serotonin** via a two-step route (C00078 → C00643 → C00780), grounding the claim that the treatment group perturbation likely begins with altered tryptophan availability or catabolism. However, neither serotonin nor L-tryptophan connects to quinolinic acid within the KEGG reaction graph under default traversal parameters, indicating that the kynurenine branch may be regulated independently or via a non-annotated intermediate.\n\nThe biological significance of this pattern is considerable. Coordinated elevation of serotonin, melatonin, and tryptamine alongside quinolinic acid and 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid suggests the treatment shifted tryptophan flux away from the classic serotonergic arm toward the kynurenine pathway. Quinolinic acid is a well-characterized NMDA receptor agonist and neurotoxin implicated in excitotoxicity, neuroinflammation, and neurodegenerative disorders, while 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid acts paradoxically as both an antioxidant and a generator of reactive oxygen species in immune cells. The simultaneous increase in these intermediates therefore points toward a pro-inflammatory or excitotoxic state in the affected tissue. The upstream relationship between L-tryptophan and serotonin — verified in the KEGG graph — anchors the pathway perturbation at the substrate level, implying that the treatment may either increase tryptophan availability or activate the enzymes (tryptophan hydroxylase, aromatic amino acid decarboxylase) that channel tryptophan toward its neurotransmitter derivatives. Further targeted profiling of upstream enzymes and downstream pathway ratios would be needed to resolve the mechanistic driver of this coordinated change.",
